#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134674} :activity/id #uuid "8da487d8-5da0-5730-90f5-0cc952b0b118" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049970 :topic/id #uuid "22979210-8cd9-5db4-861d-faf167d0a850" :topic/math-academy-id 2124} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135049 :tutorial/content "We've already seen how to find the orthogonal projection of a vector onto a subspace in the case when the subspace is defined using an orthogonal basis. In this lesson, we'll learn how to do the same thing in the case of non-orthogonal bases.\n\nFor example, consider the following vectors:\n![](images/28/28ce7470231775d8889555d0dd9f37ee9215044762a40335bd43578b121486a3.svg)\nHow can we find the orthogonal projection $\\mathbf{x}_S$ of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}?$ Notice that since $\\mathbf a_1\\cdot \\mathbf a_2 \\neq 0,$ the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are not orthogonal.\n\nAs usual, we would like to write $\\mathbf{x}$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}_S + \\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\in S$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\!\\in S^\\perp.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a5576fcaf9e0be6fc14978eb91dcbaceee75cbfddaa537fdb05d34b8e31e2e0.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =x-{x}_{S} \\\\  & =x-({k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\in S^\\perp,$ that is, $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\perp S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expression for $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ into the system to get\n![](images/f1/f1e3c7ae9e966ebcab88901c9e44a5449cb98716f43fc526d306e0af5186923b.svg)\n\nWe can write this system in an equivalent matrix form as follows:\n![](images/47/472c41e1e509d2db9c41dfa95127382eeba7ab5fbaa4172d680c39335694ef2e.svg)\n\nIf we denote \n![](images/26/2650c1020d413a7c988279cc698ab4b7c4caff427615d49b35ef4e9a42651ff0.svg)\nand ![](images/9b/9b50884bd0af404e71f9d7734e9bc054fc9439aba02780cf2beb5fa0a6ac1f32.svg) then we obtain\n\n$$\nA^TA\\mathbf{k} = A^T\\mathbf{x}\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{k}=(A^TA)^{-1}A^T\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nAlso, we note that  $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2$ can be expressed as $\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x}\t= A\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =Ak \\\\  & =A({A}^{T}A)^{-1}{A}^{T}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a formula for the orthogonal projection!\n\nNow, we return to our example. Using the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 11\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note**: Pay attention to the order in which we carried out the multiplication. Carrying out the operations in the order shown above can save us a lot of work!" :tutorial/id #uuid "0a9a954f-9338-5c3f-a9db-9b39a1a60c0b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3626 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f3cce71c-fd0d-5040-949b-813f3fdcbdd7" :step/math-academy-id 20246 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133788 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133597 :question/id #uuid "b8bbe0e1-8bf1-5b3a-a77e-6350ea77e83f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5575"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537904}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "8e0f305d-efdf-586e-b799-66fe2b5a8f6a" :step/math-academy-id 20247 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134678}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135050 :tutorial/content "If $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is a set of *linearly independent* vectors, then the orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $A$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n,$ that is,\n![](images/25/25a85721a3721ace1a32f6ed457749091452df93053d2eba7dbfaa48f6f74189.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** The fact that $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ must be linearly independent is important. If the vectors are linearly dependent, we need to find the basis of the span before applying the formula." :tutorial/id #uuid "3274de58-5861-55c3-9614-08540ad4231e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3627 :tutorial/title "The General Formula for the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "c20a2e38-a98a-579e-9564-09d78a7f10c9" :step/math-academy-id 20248 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133789 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133598 :question/id #uuid "ac8363a8-fc77-52e0-b060-5e3a45c5171a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5576"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050727}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537968}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"} :step/id #uuid "184ad4b9-e662-5d0f-9c92-14f6b77fcc1f" :step/math-academy-id 20249 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135051 :tutorial/content "As we've already seen, the orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the one-dimensional subspace $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}} \\, \\mathbf{x} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}}{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}} \\: \\mathbf{a}.\n$$\n\nIf we suppose that $\\mathbf{a}$ denotes the column-vector ![](images/c2/c23a26d5ce1137e222e28a4bc872f9bcffda2cd6430f2d5532618ccf0ed95fa5.svg) then the dot product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ is simply $\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a},$ and we can write the formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{(a\\cdot x)}{(a\\cdot a)}\\,a \\\\  & =[\\frac{({a}^{T}x)}{({a}^{T}a)}]a \\\\  & =a[\\frac{({a}^{T}x)}{({a}^{T}a)}] \\\\  & =a[({a}^{T}a)^{-1}{a}^{T}x] \\\\  & =a\\,({a}^{T}a)^{-1}{a}^{T}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compare the above with the formula that we use when we want to project onto an arbitrary subspace:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\n \nAs we can see, they are basically the same thing!" :tutorial/id #uuid "491ffb4a-98e5-59b5-8c01-3d23f872cc3f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3628 :tutorial/title "Some Intuition Behind the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "5b14c806-2358-52c9-aabe-db4086c3d1ef" :step/math-academy-id 20250}] :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Euclidean Spaces (Arbitrary Bases)" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134003}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489330} :activity/id #uuid "a5e50e5c-f6d4-570d-8bfd-dd50d0a5e6dd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049970 :topic/id #uuid "22979210-8cd9-5db4-861d-faf167d0a850" :topic/math-academy-id 2124} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d911574-ccd3-5c98-bd89-40abf5381b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488566} {:answer/id #uuid "2e54bdc8-061f-5aca-ad4c-768aadfc8c96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488570} {:answer/id #uuid "96ca6b9c-ffc7-5276-bee4-ff0be2c4198c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488774} {:answer/id #uuid "e1378868-2a9d-52dd-8e6a-ac7feee66d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488938} {:answer/id #uuid "f00d63d7-b728-5541-b6c4-ed9e9411144d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488977}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488570} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0ad7dc8-4543-5cca-8939-06979b0f56c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e47080dd-98d4-5498-967c-b6d64e7e3867" :question/math-academy-id "q-40629" :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "55b0eb69-ee74-5a65-98a4-635c7ba90856" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489378}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c5f794-99b0-5846-9311-18d5de992953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488543} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1837d2-279c-5b39-b335-7e4e449a5f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488750} {:answer/id #uuid "908d17a2-f2f1-56f9-acd8-74a95eb4695f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488758} {:answer/id #uuid "a33bde79-ffb2-5dbe-bf12-ffd8a5fc10a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488794} {:answer/id #uuid "db1a839b-51b6-5277-a632-d4ded0706f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488794} :answer-field/id #uuid "878a4e45-5d30-5f1c-aefb-67035a88d122" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c6ef098f-928d-59a3-9d4f-bfcb112bab97" :question/math-academy-id "q-39869" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 19\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 28\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -7 \\\\ -7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 28\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "772f1302-aeb0-5063-a912-a0f326d01ac2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489353}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489337 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1b1ee61-1308-5d61-b7ed-da45b7cfca5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388969} {:answer/id #uuid "f88bb249-b083-5351-9669-33db5c6b2547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388970} {:answer/id #uuid "d77cc695-36e6-5192-b27f-3e733685eb86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388971} {:answer/id #uuid "55a3f1da-3779-59ce-ae0d-0dbecdb726b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388972} {:answer/id #uuid "053ae894-bb63-5ac6-b828-d9ea6bb6af7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388973}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388972} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa6cde22-2f73-519a-945c-bffdd259e21b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dd7403df-a496-59c6-8226-c07a3e6dd8d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-158123" :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8bf7a2d1-6483-574a-bd3f-c2612cb3dafd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f402a3a-4f08-5c72-a337-37b1173065e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488572} {:answer/id #uuid "69d00f66-bac7-5a07-842c-86c8ae454700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488678} {:answer/id #uuid "795fe3b9-08f5-5072-9238-46b50ec1f87a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488709} {:answer/id #uuid "924789e2-65b9-582c-b2a0-b829c5928e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488762} {:answer/id #uuid "94182863-ea1a-5c03-b764-976abf2d1657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488768}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488678} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae069ad-82b0-5089-9f78-df2bcd3c3331" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "86358ef9-78e9-5caa-a7a3-a7c614e96d69" :question/math-academy-id "q-40628" :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ad4ab2c8-0a51-5f88-9dd3-02a2bc3345a4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489378 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4de82d8c-ce23-555b-bc98-20cd4b7e3d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394740} {:answer/id #uuid "9d838a17-fcf6-5cd8-b98f-01a452dd4e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394741} {:answer/id #uuid "931f8ee6-6ee9-5feb-989f-f94e1f389d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394742} {:answer/id #uuid "134407e9-0936-586f-9d57-4a275793781d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :